Read a found store value under the lock (MuPDF commit 5ba5c97, backported to 1.28.5)

This file modifies MuPDF. It is Artifex's fix for MuPDF bug 709849,
commit 5ba5c976427aa2c084d21e43e71f5f8d4eb87105 on MuPDF master ("Bug
709849: Fix race condition on free/read.", Robin Watts, committed on
2026-10-20), with its changed lines applied unchanged to
source/fitz/store.c of the 1.28.5 source release. The Mervin PDF project
added it on 2026-10-20 in place of its own patch of 2026-10-18.
scripts/build-mupdf-linux.sh and scripts/build-mupdf-windows.ps1 apply
it before building. Delete it when Mervin updates to a MuPDF release
that contains 5ba5c97.

fz_find_item and the duplicate branch of fz_store_item take a reference
to the value of the entry they found, release FZ_LOCK_ALLOC, and only
then read item->val from the fz_item again. fz_filter_store,
fz_remove_item and fz_empty_store unlink and free an fz_item whatever
its value's reference count, and MuPDF calls fz_filter_store itself
when a PDF closes (pdf_empty_store and fz_drop_drawn_tiles_for_document).
If another thread frees the entry between the unlock and the read, the
function returns a pointer read from freed memory.

In Mervin, render workers rasterise display lists outside the document
lock, and the draw device looks up and stores pattern tiles there
(fz_find_item and fz_store_item in draw-device.c). Closing a tab drops
its document on another thread, which filters those tiles out of the
store.

The commit copies the value into a local while the lock is held and
returns the local. The reference taken under the lock keeps the value
alive after the entry is freed. foundValueSurvivesEviction in
tests/tst_mupdf_store.cpp checks it. fz_store_item has a second window,
while it makes room for a new entry, which patch 0003 closes.

diff --git a/source/fitz/store.c b/source/fitz/store.c
index dd0640b..171548a 100644
--- a/source/fitz/store.c
+++ b/source/fitz/store.c
@@ -502,6 +502,7 @@ fz_store_item(fz_context *ctx, void *key, void *val_, size_t itemsize, const fz_
 		{
 			/* There was one there already! Take a new reference
 			 * to the existing one, and drop our current one. */
+			fz_storable *existing_val;
 			fz_warn(ctx, "found duplicate %s in the store", type->name);
 			touch(store, existing);
 			if (existing->val->refs > 0)
@@ -509,10 +510,14 @@ fz_store_item(fz_context *ctx, void *key, void *val_, size_t itemsize, const fz_
 				(void)Memento_takeRef(existing->val);
 				existing->val->refs++;
 			}
+			/* Read existing_val while still locked. val is now protected by
+			 * our reference. existing is not, so item could be freed before
+			 * we return. */
+			existing_val = existing->val;
 			fz_unlock(ctx, FZ_LOCK_ALLOC);
 			fz_free(ctx, item);
 			type->drop_key(ctx, key);
-			return existing->val;
+			return (void *)existing_val;
 		}
 	}
 
@@ -617,6 +622,7 @@ fz_find_item(fz_context *ctx, fz_store_drop_fn *drop, void *key, const fz_store_
 		 * picked up from the hash before it has made it into the
 		 * linked list does not get whipped out again due to the
 		 * store being full. */
+		fz_storable *item_val;
 		touch(store, item);
 		/* And bump the refcount before returning */
 		if (item->val->refs > 0)
@@ -624,8 +630,12 @@ fz_find_item(fz_context *ctx, fz_store_drop_fn *drop, void *key, const fz_store_
 			(void)Memento_takeRef(item->val);
 			item->val->refs++;
 		}
+		/* Read item_val while still locked. val is now protected by
+		 * our reference. item is not, so item could be freed before
+		 * we return. */
+		item_val = item->val;
 		fz_unlock(ctx, FZ_LOCK_ALLOC);
-		return (void *)item->val;
+		return (void *)item_val;
 	}
 	fz_unlock(ctx, FZ_LOCK_ALLOC);
 
